n"],"presenter":null,"image":"0Vu5Uuz38dT4t30GYNeZ2A","website":"https://assembly.ca","tickets":null},{"id":"c9d7ff36-710e-477c-ae8c-25d0553e709d","name":"Free Night at the Museum","dates":[1761076800000,1763499600000,1765918800000,1768942800000,1771362000000,1773777600000,1776801600000,1779220800000,1781640000000,1784664000000,1787083200000,1789502400000,1792526400000],"start":"2025-10-21T16:00:00-04:00","end":"2026-10-20T16:00:00-04:00","allDay":"Day Event","venue":{"name":"Royal Ontario Museum","address":"100 Queen's Park, Toronto, ON, M5S 2C6","lat":43.667795,"lng":-79.394212},"free":true,"low":null,"high":null,"pwyc":null,"costNotes":"Click the \"Book Tickets\" button at the top of the webpage. Then, select the 3rd Tuesday of the month on the calendar, and check the box for “3rd Tuesday Nights Free.” \n\nNote: Tickets are released 2 weeks prior to the event.","description":"Access to most galleries will be free for everyone, on the third Tuesday Night of each month!\n","categories":["Cultural","History","Museum"],"presenter":"Royal Ontario Museum","image":"HL82fxGUvbszy_QZLUul-Q","website":"https://www.rom.on.ca/whats-on/special-programs/third-tuesday-nights-free?utm_source=cityoftoronto&utm_medium=listing&utm_campaign=ttnf&utm_content=tourism","tickets":"https://www.rom.on.ca/whats-on/special-programs/third-tuesday-nights-free?utm_source=cityoftoronto&utm_medium=listing&utm_campaign=ttnf&utm_content=tourism"},{"id":"7ed2572e-45e5-4318-be30-c3035d23be9e","name":"Free Night at the Museum","dates":[1761076800000,1763499600000,1765918800000,1768942800000,1771362000000,1773777600000,1776801600000,1779220800000,1781640000000,1784664000000,1787083200000,1789502400000,1792526400000],"start":"2025-10-21T16:00:00-04:00","end":"2026-10-20T16:00:00-04:00","allDay":"Day Event","venue":{"name":"Royal Ontario Museum","address":"100 Queen's Park, Toronto, ON, M5S 2C6","lat":43.667795,"lng":-79.394212},"free":true,"low":null,"high":null,"pwyc":null,"costNotes":"Click the \"Book Tickets\" button at the top of the webpage. Then, select the 3rd Tuesday of the month on the calendar, and check the box for “3rd Tuesday Nights Free.” \n\nNote: Tickets are released 2 weeks prior to the event.","description":"Access to most galleries will be free for everyone, on the third Tuesday Night of each month!\n","categories":["Cultural","History","Museum"],"presenter":"Royal Ontario Museum","image":"HL82fxGUvbszy_QZLUul-Q","website":"https://www.rom.on.ca/whats-on/special-programs/third-tuesday-nights-free?utm_source=cityoftoronto&utm_medium=listing&utm_campaign=ttnf&utm_content=tourism","tickets":"https://www.rom.on.ca/whats-on/special-programs/third-tuesday-nights-free?utm_source=cityoftoronto&utm_medium=listing&utm_campaign=ttnf&utm_content=tourism"},{"id":"43f15f49-b72e-4b3a-868b-1ff4be6287e3","name":"Molly Steels: I Am/Was/Always","dates":[1793199600000,1793289600000,1793376000000,1793462400000,1793811600000,1793898000000,1793984400000,1794070800000,1794416400000,1794502800000,1794589200000,1794675600000,1795021200000,1795107600000,1795194000000,1795280400000,1795626000000,1795712400000,1795798800000,1795885200000,1796230800000,1796317200000,1796403600000,1796490000000],"start":"2026-10-28T11:00:00-04:00","end":"2026-12-05T12:00:00-05:00","allDay":"Day Event","venue":{"name":"The Image Centre","address":"33 Gould St, Toronto, ON, M5B 1E9","lat":43.657662387,"lng":-79.379262289},"free":true,"low":null,"high":null,"pwyc":null,"costNotes":null,"description":"The philosopher Diana Prokofyeva considers a photograph to be a phenomenon that exists as a way to avoid death by copying a moment and suspending it within time - a way to be between two worlds:, the living and the dead. She suggests that a photograph gives confidence of existence, a way to state, “Here I am and was, and always shall have been.”.\n\nView this evolving series that looks to photography as a method of conceptualizing grief and mortality. Using family archives from the past century and conceptual imagery, I locate myself within a chain of human experience. I look to the women who came before me, observing the proof of their existence with reverence and the knowledge that I will one day add to this catalogue. Through this series I aim to overshadow existential fear with curiosity.\n\nEphemeral aspects of nature are woven into this work as both an unrelenting force and a source of comfort. ","categories":["Arts/Exhibits","Cultural","Museum"],"presenter":null,"image":"oUxX7buKjY1V2s_fjb6MLg","website":"https://theimagecentre.ca/exhibition/molly-steels-iamwasalways/","tickets":null},{"id":"36e331c1-3520-4df3-8685-e9897bf466db","name":"Molly Steels: I Am/Was/Always","dates":[1793199600000,1793289600000,1793376000000,1793462400000,1793811600000,1793898000000,1793984400000,1794070800000,1794416400000,1794502800000,1794589200000,1794675600000,1795021200000,1795107600000,1795194000000,1795280400000,1795626000000,1795712400000,1795798800000,1795885200000,1796230800000,1796317200000,1796403600000,1796490000000],"start":"2026-10-28T11:00:00-04:00","end":"2026-12-05T12:00:00-05:00","allDay":"Day Event","venue":{"name":"The Image Centre","address":"33 Gould St, Toronto, ON, M5B 1E9","lat":43.657662387,"lng":-79.379262289},"free":true,"low":null,"high":null,"pwyc":null,"costNotes":null,"description":"The philosopher Diana Prokofyeva considers a photograph to be a phenomenon that exists as a way to avoid death by copying a moment and suspending it within time - a way to be between two worlds:, the living and the dead. She suggests that a photograph gives confidence of existence, a way to state, “Here I am and was, and always shall have been.”.\n\nView this evolving series that looks to photography as a method of conceptualizing grief and mortality. Using family archives from the past century and conceptual imagery, I locate myself within a chain of human experience. I look to the women who came before me, observing the proof of their existence with reverence and the knowledge that I will one day add to this catalogue. Through this series I aim to overshadow existential fear with curiosity.\n\nEphemeral aspects of nature are woven into this work as both an unrelenting force and a source of comfort. ","categories":["Arts/Exhibits","Cultural","Museum"],"presenter":null,"image":"oUxX7buKjY1V2s_fjb6MLg","website":"https://theimagecentre.ca/exhibition/molly-steels-iamwasalways/","tickets":null},{"id":"1f94e410-19d2-411a-819b-0aca99ad7a42","name":"Molly Steels: I Am/Was/Always","dates":[1793199600000,1793289600000,1793376000000,1793462400000,1793811600000,1793898000000,1793984400000,1794070800000,1794416400000,1794502800000,1794589200000,1794675600000,1795021200000,1795107600000,1795194000000,1795280400000,1795626000000,1795712400000,1795798800000,1795885200000,1796230800000,1796317200000,1796403600000,1796490000000],"start":"2026-10-28T11:00:00-04:00","end":"2026-12-05T12:00:00-05:00","allDay":"Day Event","venue":{"name":"The Image Centre","address":"33 Gould St, Toronto, ON, M5B 1E9","lat":43.657662387,"lng":-79.379262289},"free":true,"low":null,"high":null,"pwyc":null,"costNotes":null,"description":"The philosopher Diana Prokofyeva considers a photograph to be a phenomenon that exists as a way to avoid death by copying a moment and suspending it within time - a way to be between two worlds:, the living and the dead. She suggests that a photograph gives confidence of existence, a way to state, “Here I am and was, and always shall have been.”.\n\nView this evolving series that looks to photography as a method of conceptualizing grief and mortality. Using family archives from the past century and conceptual imagery, I locate myself within a chain of human experience. I look to the women who came before me, observing the proof of their existence with reverence and the knowledge that I will one day add to this catalogue. Through this series I aim to overshadow existential fear with curiosity.\n\nEphemeral aspects of nature are wove