in Germany where no Jews remain to a Jahalin Bedouin community in Khan al-Ahmar; from the contested streets of Jerusalem to the fraught landscapes of Ontario. ","categories":["Arts/Exhibits","Cultural","Talks"],"presenter":"Fentster Gallery","image":"T7PtQvwfAsgTqCPeW8LzYw","website":"https://fentster.org/birds-nest","tickets":null},{"id":"c6ddc20e-2c56-4708-a8e1-9322764eb241","name":"Sala-Manca Group: Like a Bird’s Nest","dates":[1790204400000,1790269200000,1790352000000,1790438400000,1790524800000,1790787600000,1790874000000,1790956800000,1791043200000,1791129600000,1791392400000,1791478800000,1791561600000,1791648000000,1791734400000,1791997200000,1792083600000,1792166400000,1792252800000,1792339200000,1792602000000,1792688400000,1792771200000,1792857600000,1792944000000,1793206800000,1793293200000,1793376000000,1793462400000,1793552400000,1793815200000,1793901600000,1793984400000,1794070800000,1794157200000,1794420000000,1794506400000,1794589200000,1794675600000,1794762000000,1795024800000,1795111200000,1795194000000,1795280400000,1795366800000,1795629600000,1795716000000,1795798800000,1795885200000,1795971600000],"start":"2026-09-23T19:00:00-04:00","end":"2026-11-29T12:00:00-05:00","allDay":"Day Event","venue":{"name":"PIX FILM Gallery","address":"1411 Dufferin St, Toronto, ON, M6H 4C7","lat":43.670941,"lng":-79.439848},"free":true,"low":null,"high":null,"pwyc":null,"costNotes":null,"description":"Enjoy a landmark retrospective from the international artist collective, the Sala-Manca Group.\n\nInstallation, video and photography are exhibited together for the first time, all connected to the ritual object of the sukkah, a temporary hut traditionally erected during the Jewish festival of Sukkot.\n\nOver the last twenty years, Sala-Manca has explored the symbolism and function of the sukkah and related themes of shelter, impermanence, displacement, and liberation. In their hands, the sukkah is interchangeably a ritual structure, an ethnographic object, a work of art, an ephemeral monument, a site of protest, and a performative arena. \n\nThis exhibition traverses unlikely geographies, travelling through time and place, from a small town in Germany where no Jews remain to a Jahalin Bedouin community in Khan al-Ahmar; from the contested streets of Jerusalem to the fraught landscapes of Ontario. ","categories":["Arts/Exhibits","Cultural","Talks"],"presenter":"Fentster Gallery","image":"T7PtQvwfAsgTqCPeW8LzYw","website":"https://fentster.org/birds-nest","tickets":null},{"id":"7330ae09-8c07-4400-9804-558c0f03923b","name":"Strings Plus! The Lady with the Hammer","dates":[1800664200000],"start":"2027-01-22T19:30:00-05:00","end":"2027-01-22T19:30:00-05:00","allDay":"Night Event","venue":{"name":"Trinity- St. Paul's Centre","address":"427 Bloor St W, Toronto, ON, M5S 1X7","lat":43.66603939,"lng":-79.405707818},"free":false,"low":null,"high":null,"pwyc":null,"costNotes":null,"description":"Open the new year with two remarkable soloists and a program built around the drama and drive of timpani. \n\nPianist Élisabeth Pion, Gold Laureate of the 2025 Honens International Piano Competition and a Radio-Canada Breakthrough Artist joins Toronto's own violinist Julia Mirzoev, named one of CBC's Top 30 Classical Musicians Under 30. Enjoy a evening with sounds of Bach's radiant Violin Concerto in E Major to the fierce intensity of Ustvolskaya's Piano Concerto for Strings and Timpani, receiving its Toronto premiere.\n\nNorbert Palej's \"Song of Smyrna\" and Edvard Mirzoyan's Symphony for Strings and Timpani complete a program of exceptional depth and power, all under the baton of Maestro Nurhan Arman.\n\nTwo soloists. Two premieres. A beautiful start to the new year.\n","categories":["Cultural","Live Performances","Music"],"presenter":"Sinfonia Toronto","image":"GaiQZxGWaxsyYTkdAiIJDQ","website":"https://www.sinfoniatoronto.com/2026-2027-season/strings-plus","tickets":"https://www.sinfoniatoronto.com/2026-2027-season/strings-plus"},{"id":"f7fa93a4-1433-47b4-b99d-bd214156a7fd","name":"Wonderland Road by Carrianne Leung – By the Lake Book Club","dates":[1828224000000],"start":"2027-12-07T19:00:00-05:00","end":"2027-12-07T19:00:00-05:00","allDay":"Night Event","venue":{"name":"Assembly Hall","address":"1 Colonel Samuel Smith Park Dr, Etobicoke, ON, M8V 4B6","lat":43.597660441,"lng":-79.515824559},"free":false,"low":10,"high":21,"pwyc":null,"costNotes":null,"description":"Presented by: City of Toronto’s Assembly Hall, Humber Polytechnic’s Faculty of Media, Creative Arts and Design\n\nWonderland Road is a dystopian novel set in a near future shaped by uncertainty, where three lives intertwine in a neighbourhood struggling to endure loss, isolation and change. Through characters searching for safety and connection, Carrianne Leung explores survival, community, family and hope in an unpredictable world. In conversation, she'll speak about the ideas that sparked the novel and how resilience, compassion and human connection emerge throughout the story.\n\nBy the Lake Book Club offers six Tuesday-night events, between February and December featuring leading Canadian authors in conversation with Humber Polytechnics alumni. Each in-person event includes a reading, discussion, audience Q&A, book-signing and American sign language interpretation. Virtual events are subtitled live.\n","categories":["Literary","Talks"],"presenter":null,"image":"e8RR4B7wlgk8ioYlvAucQQ","website":"https://toronto.ca/BookClub","tickets":"https://secure.toronto.ca/webapps/feps_edc-ticketing/#events/084a6d2b-8730-4643-90e0-9a2be6b977ec"},{"id":"347cd4bb-2ba9-4909-9d5c-9c5fcc1c052e","name":"Retracing Tatreez: Palestinian Thobes through the Twentieth Century","dates":[1790265600000,1790352000000,1790438400000,1790524800000,1790784000000,1790870400000,1790956800000,1791043200000,1791129600000,1791388800000,1791475200000,1791561600000,1791648000000,1791734400000,1791993600000,1792080000000,1792166400000,1792252800000,1792339200000,1792598400000,17