fashion history, cultural sustainability, and decolonial design practices, the exhibition highlights how contemporary Indigenous couturiers blend ancestral knowledge with cutting-edge innovation to redefine fashion on their own terms.\n\n","categories":["Arts/Exhibits","Cultural","Museum"],"presenter":null,"image":"azAuMTKhii0Yt5PF3WwMYg","website":"https://textilemuseum.ca/event/always-in-fashion/","tickets":"https://textilemuseumcanada.ticketing.veevartapp.com/tickets/view/calendar"},{"id":"6fbc8dfa-1890-4414-844e-b30cfd50e92e","name":"Always In Fashion","dates":[1789574400000,1789660800000,1789747200000,1789833600000,1789920000000,1790179200000,1790265600000,1790352000000,1790438400000,1790524800000,1790784000000,1790870400000,1790956800000,1791043200000,1791129600000,1791388800000,1791475200000,1791561600000,1791648000000,1791734400000,1791993600000,1792080000000,1792166400000,1792252800000,1792339200000,1792598400000,1792684800000,1792771200000,1792857600000,1792944000000,1793203200000,1793289600000,1793376000000,1793462400000,1793552400000,1793811600000,1793898000000,1793984400000,1794070800000,1794157200000,1794416400000,1794502800000,1794589200000,1794675600000,1794762000000,1795021200000,1795107600000,1795194000000,1795280400000,1795366800000,1795626000000,1795712400000,1795798800000,1795885200000,1795971600000,1796230800000,1796317200000,1796403600000,1796490000000,1796576400000,1796835600000,1796922000000,1797008400000,1797094800000,1797181200000,1797440400000,1797526800000,1797613200000,1797699600000,1797786000000,1798045200000,1798131600000,1798218000000,1798304400000,1798390800000,1798650000000,1798736400000,1798822800000,1798909200000,1798995600000],"start":"2026-09-16T12:00:00-04:00","end":"2027-01-03T12:00:00-05:00","allDay":"Day Event","venue":{"name":"Textile Museum of Canada","address":"55 Centre Ave, Toronto, ON, M5G 2H5","lat":43.654578756,"lng":-79.386652476},"free":false,"low":null,"high":null,"pwyc":null,"costNotes":null,"description":"Curated by Amber-Dawn Bear Robe, enjoy an immersive exploration of Indigenous design languages and their transformative impact on the fashion landscape of the lands we call Canada and the United States. \n\nThis exhibition emerges from the urgent need to recognize and celebrate Indigenous designers who are reshaping the industry by reclaiming cultural narratives, innovating with ancestral techniques, and challenging colonial frameworks within fashion. \n\nThrough research on Indigenous fashion history, cultural sustainability, and decolonial design practices, the exhibition highlights how contemporary Indigenous couturiers blend ancestral knowledge with cutting-edge innovation to redefine fashion on their own terms.\n\n","categories":["Arts/Exhibits","Cultural","Museum"],"presenter":null,"image":"azAuMTKhii0Yt5PF3WwMYg","website":"https://textilemuseum.ca/event/always-in-fashion/","tickets":"https://textilemuseumcanada.ticketing.veevartapp.com/tickets/view/calendar"},{"id":"0c0d65bd-aaca-4f68-8101-15bd22218e3a","name":"Always In Fashion","dates":[1789574400000,1789660800000,1789747200000,1789833600000,1789920000000,1790179200000,1790265600000,1790352000000,1790438400000,1790524800000,1790784000000,1790870400000,1790956800000,1791043200000,1791129600000,1791388800000,1791475200000,1791561600000,1791648000000,1791734400000,1791993600000,1792080000000,1792166400000,1792252800000,1792339200000,1792598400000,1792684800000,1792771200000,1792857600000,1792944000000,1793203200000,1793289600000,1793376000000,1793462400000,1793552400000,1793811600000,1793898000000,1793984400000,1794070800000,1794157200000,1794416400000,1794502800000,1794589200000,1794675600000,1794762000000,1795021200000,1795107600000,1795194000000,1795280400000,1795366800000,1795626000000,1795712400000,1795798800000,1795885200000,1795971600000,1796230800000,1796317200000,1796403600000,1796490000000,1796576400000,1796835600000,1796922000000,1797008400000,1797094800000,1797181200000,1797440400000,1797526800000,1797613200000,1797699600000,1797786000000,1798045200000,1798131600000,1798218000000,1798304400000,1798390800000,1798650000000,1798736400000,1798822800000,1798909200000,1798995600000],"start":"2026-09-16T12:00:00-04:00","end":"2027-01-03T12:00:00-05:00","allDay":"Day Event","venue":{"name":"Textile Museum of Canada","address":"55 Centre Ave, Toronto, ON, M5G 2H5","lat":43.654578756,"lng":-79.386652476},"free":false,"low":null,"high":null,"pwyc":null,"costNotes":null,"description":"Curated by Amber-Dawn Bear Robe, enjoy an immersive exploration of Indigenous design languages and their transformative impact on the fashion landscape of the lands we call Canada and the United States. \n\nThis exhibition emerges from the urgent need to recognize and celebrate Indigenous designers who are reshaping the industry by reclaiming cultural narratives, innovating with ancestral techniques, and challenging colonial frameworks within fashion. \n\nThrough research on Indigenous fashion history, cultural sustainability, and decolonial design practices, the exhibition highlights how contemporary Indigenous couturiers blend ancestral knowledge with cutting-edge innovation to redefine fashion on their own terms.\n\n","categories":["Arts/Exhibits","Cultural","Museum"],"presenter":null,"image":"azAuMTKhii0Yt5PF3WwMYg","website":"https://textilemuseum.ca/event/always-in-fashion/","tickets":"https://textilemuseumcanada.ticketing.veevartapp.com/tickets/view/calendar"},{"id":"3c71bfb5-4d0d-411c-9564-518d6459b4e0","name":"“Dakiimi / To have land“ by Amber Quail","dates":[1790445600000,1790690400000,1790776800000,1790863200000,1790949600000,1791036000000,1791295200000],"start":"2026-09-26T14:00:00-04:00","end":"2026-10-06T10:00:00-04:00","allDay":"Day Event","venue":{"name":"Riverdale Hub","address":"1326 Gerrard St E, Toronto, ON, M4L 1Z1","lat":43.671781866,"lng":-79.32550473},"free":true,"low":null,"high":null,"pwyc":null,"costNotes":null,"description":"Iconography in Western culture has been a central theme in Amber’s visual arts practice. Familiar household archetypes, reimagined through an Indigenous lens, invite viewers to consider alternate truths beyond those presented by the dominant culture.\n\nAn off-reserve member of Alderville First Nation, Amber’s practice is based in Tkaronto and South Algonquin. In Dakiimi / To Have Land, the iconic board game Monopoly serves as both an anchor and a catalyst for reflection on land theft and greed. Through vibrant beadwork, mixed-media works, and paintings, Amber explores land, ownership, power and the enduring impacts of colonialism.","categories":["Arts/Exhibits","Indigenous"],"presenter":"Riverdale Hub Gallery","image":null,"website":"https://riverdalehub.ca/dakiimi-to-have-land-an-exhibition-by-amber-smith-quail/","tickets":"https://riverdalehub.ca/gallery-event-registration/"},{"id":"9327ec7d-6054-4317-920e-f9c11146c8bf","name":"“Dakiimi / To have land“ by Amber Quail","dates":[1790445600000,1790690400000,1790776800000,1790863200000,1790949600000,1791036000000,1791295200000],"start":"2026-09-26T14:00:00-04:00","end":"2026-10-06T10:00:00-04:00","allDay":"Day Event","venue":{"name":"Riverdale Hub","address":"1326 Gerrard St E, Toronto, ON, M4L 1Z1","lat":43.671781866,"lng":-79.32550473},"free":true,"low":null,"high":null,"pwyc":null,"costNotes":null,"description":"Iconography in Western culture has been a central theme in Amber’s visual arts practice. Familiar household archetypes, reimagined through an Indigenous lens, invite viewers to consider alternate truths beyond those presented by the dominant culture.\n\nAn off-reserve member of Alderville First Nation, Amber’s practice is based in Tkaronto and South Algonquin. In Dakiimi / To Have Land, the iconic board game Monopoly serves as both an anchor and a catalyst for reflection on land theft and greed. Through vibrant beadwork, mixed-media works, and paintings, Amber explores land, ownership, power and the enduring impacts of colonialism.","categories":["Arts/Exhibits","Indigenous"],"presenter":"Riverdale Hub Gallery","image":null,"website":"https://riverdalehub.ca/dakiimi-to-have-land-an-exhibition-by-amber-smith-quail/","tickets":"https://riverdalehub.ca/gallery-event-registration/"},{"id":"9797db4f-1f00-4bef-ac8c-a143d3dda86a","name":"Lassonde Art Trail: Art + Nature Tour","dates":[1789228800000,1789833600000,1790438400000,1791043200000,1791648000000,1792252800000,1792857600000,1793462400000,1794070800000,1794675600000],"start":"2026-09-12T12:00:00-04:00","end":"2026-11-14T12:00:00-05:00","allDay":"Day Event","venue":{"name":"Biidaasige Park ","address":"51 Commissioners St, Toronto, ON, M5A 1A6","lat":43.646495807,"lng":-79.348925214},"free":true,"low":null,"high":null,"pwyc":null,"costNotes":null,"description":"Join LAT every Saturday at 12 pm or 2 pm for a drop-in Art + Nature tour led by a LAT Art Guide. These walking tours invite participants to explore the artworks along the Lassonde Art Trail while discovering the rich history, ecology, and ongoing transformation of Biidaasige Park.\n\n12 pm - Art + Nature Tour (30 minutes, slow paced)\n2 pm - Art + Nature Tour (1 hour)\nMeet in front of Old Fire Hall 30. All tours are drop-in and free to attend. Please be aware that the trees in the park system are not yet mature, so there is little shade. We recommend dressing according to the weather. Tours are subject to cancellation in the event of adverse weather. Please see Lassonde Art Trail website for further details.","categories":["Arts/Exhibits","Environmental","Tour"],"presenter":null,"image":"Xy_GPUCC1IStLKdFhFwxCg","website":"https://lassondearttrail.org/art-trail/","tickets":null},{"id":"762f3b2f-db38-4eb4-8259-b0a64f87b127","name":"Sala-Manca Group: Like a Bird’s Nest","dates":[1790204400000,1790269200000,1790352000000,1790438400000,1790524800000,1790787600000,1790874000000,1790956800000,1791043200000,1791129600000,1791392400000,1791478800000,1791561600000,1791648000000,1791734400000,1791997200000,1792083600000,1792166400000,1792252800000,1792339200000,1792602000000,1792688400000,1792771200000,1792857600000,1792944000000,1793206800000,1793293200000,1793376000000,1793462400000,1793552400000,1793815200000,1793901600000,1793984400000,1794070800000,1794157200000,1794420000000,1794506400000,1794589200000,1794675600000,1794762000000,1795024800000,1795111200000,1795194000000,1795280400000,1795366800000,1795629600000,1795716000000,1795798800000,1795885200000,1795971600000],"start":"2026-09-23T19:00:00-04:00","end":"2026-11-29T12:00:00-05:00","allDay":"Day Event","venue":{"name":"PIX FILM Gallery","address":"1411 Dufferin St, Toronto, ON, M6H 4C7","lat":43.670941,"lng":-79.439848},"free":true,"low":null,"high":null,"pwyc":null,"costNotes":null,"description":"Enjoy a landmark retrospective from the international artist collective, the Sala-Manca Group.\n\nInstallation, video and photography are exhibited together for the first time, all connected to the ritual object of the sukkah, a temporary hut traditionally erected during the Jewish festival of Sukkot.\n\nOver the last twenty years, Sala-Manca has explored the symbolism and function of the sukkah and related themes of shelter, impermanence, displacement, and liberation. In their hands, the sukkah is interchangeably a ritual structure, an ethnographic object, a work of art, an ephemeral monument, a site of protest, and a performative arena. \n\nThis exhibition traverses unlikely geographies, travelling through time and place, from a small town in Germany where no Jews remain to a Jahalin Bedouin community in Khan al-Ahmar; from the contested streets of Jerusalem to the fraught landscapes of Ontario. ","categories":["Arts/Exhibits","Cultural","Talks"],"presenter":"Fentster Gallery","image":"T7PtQvwfAsgTqCPeW8LzYw","website":"https://fentster.org/birds-nest","tickets":null},{"id":"1f6a8505-ea76-4246-9909-9d7dc4585f2b","name":"Sala-Manca Group: Like a Bird’s Nest","dates":[1790204400000,1790269200000,1790352000000,1790438400